00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.350
Zoe: So I think the key things I've
done to change my communication style

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:15.500
is using very literal language rather
than adding in any sarcasm or phrases

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.210
that wouldn't make sense literally.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.000
Kerry: It's about all forms
of communication being valid.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:26.730
So we've got pod books, we've got pecs
books, we've got other forms of AAC.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:34.950
We're trialing iPods, which is like a
point, tap and it says on behalf of you

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.840
or the person that you're supporting.

00:00:38.709 --> 00:00:42.450
We've got choose boards
around the classroom.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.140
We've got visual aids to
communicate how we might be feeling.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.740
Zoe: Also using visuals where possible
and thinking about my body language.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.670
So if I am trying to get something
across with my facial expressions

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:59.070
or my gestures, I'd also try and
explain that to the child verbally.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:05.580
Zoe T: By changing my communication style
from an imperative style of communication

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.950
to a declarative style of communication.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:13.229
So that means that I'm taking the
pressure off the autistic person

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:16.020
by not making a demand on them.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.550
I'm not issuing commands.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.020
I'm not asking questions.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.570
I'm making declarative statements and
enabling them to, and then pausing.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.050
So make a declarative statement,
pause and wait, wait for that

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.360
autistic person to respond or not.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.410
If they don't respond, then move
on or do something different.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:38.370
But I would have been doing that
in the context of the relationship

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.050
that we have between each other.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:41.960
Phil: You know, really
reduce the language.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.350
And that's one thing that is easy to
forget, especially if you're working

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.790
with an individual who might present
as they understand what you're saying,

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:54.420
or, you know, might, you might be
able to converse or maybe even have a

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.690
good rapport with, but it's so easy to
forget that actually they might not be

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.110
processing what I'm saying, they might
just be giving a reaction that they

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.530
know is sort of socially acceptable.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.080
You know, the social norm is expected.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.640
Kerry: So yeah, we're trying to be
a communication total classroom.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:19.395
So any form of communication, whether it's
grab the hand of the person, of the adult,

00:02:19.395 --> 00:02:23.415
and take them to the thing that you want,
or whether it's a full sentence supported

00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:32.405
